= Recency effect =

== Overview ==
The '''recency effect''' is an advantage for information encountered late in a sequence.

== Human cognition ==
In immediate free recall, the final items in a list are often recalled particularly well. The magnitude of recency depends on task and delay conditions.

== Conversation and judgment ==
Recent information can also receive disproportionate weight in judgments, although not every preference for recent evidence is irrational: recent evidence may genuinely be more relevant in changing environments.

== Language models ==
LLMs can favor later answer options or later context. Controlled permutations are needed to distinguish recency from semantic preference and other prompt effects.

== References ==
* Bennet B. Murdock Jr., “The Serial Position Effect of Free Recall” (1962).
* Glanzer & Cunitz, “Two Storage Mechanisms in Free Recall”, ''Journal of Verbal Learning and Verbal Behavior'' 5 (1966): 351–360.
* Mina et al., “Cognitive Biases, Task Complexity, and Result Interpretability in Large Language Models”, COLING 2025.

